# SYNOPSIS

```usage
sponge file
```

# DESCRIPTION

`sponge` reads standard input to its end, then writes all of it to <file>, replacing what <file> held.
Since nothing is written until the input is done, a pipeline may read a file and end in `sponge` on the same file.

The input is kept in a temporary file in `/tmp`, removed as soon as it is made, so it needs `/tmp` and room there for the whole input.
A <file> that does not exist is created, asking for mode 0666; an existing one is truncated and keeps its mode.

`sponge` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/sponge`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/sponge`.
There is no native `sponge`.
It is not a POSIX command.

# SEE ALSO

tee(1), tmpfs(4); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/sponge.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Messages go to standard error, after the name `sponge` was run by: `sponge: mkstemp:` and the error when the temporary file cannot be made, `creat` <file>`:` when <file> cannot be, and `read <stdin>:`, `write <tmpfile>:`, `read <tmpfile>:` or `write` <file>`:` when copying fails.
Each exits 1.
A flag, or any number of operands but one, prints the usage message and exits 1.

# BUGS

<file> is truncated before it is written: a write that fails part way leaves it holding only part of the input, and the rest is lost with the temporary file.

`sponge` is untested on VectraOS.
